            <ArticleTitle>Neonatal bartter syndrome in an extremely low birth weight baby.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1162-1164</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4103/1319-2442.215121</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Early diagnosis of Bartter syndrome (BS) in the neonatal period is a clinical challenge, more so in an extremely low birth weight (ELBW) baby because of the inherent renal immaturity and the associated difficulty in fluid management. However, once a diagnosis is made, the disorder is known to respond well to fluid and electrolyte management, prostaglandin inhibitors, and potassium-sparing diuretics. Herein, we report a case of neonatal BS in a very premature ELBW infant.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
